From: DashBerry <zero/README.md>
Subject: [PATCH] uvc: an errored buffer is a dropped frame, not a disconnect

uvc_v4l2_video_process() read V4L2_BUF_FLAG_ERROR on a dequeued UVC buffer as
"the host is going away": it latched uvc_shutdown_requested, printed "Possible
USB shutdown requested from Host", and RETURNED WITHOUT RE-QUEUEING THE
BUFFER.

That inference was always thin, and once f_uvc was taught to survive a missed
isochronous slot it became wrong. A missed slot sets UVC_QUEUE_DROP_INCOMPLETE
and the buffer completes with an error — normal, expected, and nothing to do
with a disconnect.

The leak is what killed the stream. Each dropped frame permanently removed one
buffer from the rotation, so the pool drained one error at a time until there
was nothing left to fill, and the host got:

    poll error 1: Invalid argument (22)
    Failed to allocate a buffer

Measured on an unchanged card: 8 buffers allocated, seven "Possible USB
shutdown" lines over 23 seconds, then that error.

This is also why results were INCONSISTENT with nothing rebuilt between runs.
How long a capture lasted was only how long it took to miss enough slots, and
that varies with scene content, USB timing and temperature — the same mode
gave 4.17 s, 12.93 s, 14.16 s and 18.38 s on one image. It is why raising the
pool from 2 to 8 lengthened runs without fixing them: it bought more errors
before the same end, which read as a partial fix and was not one.

A genuine disconnect still arrives by the two routes that mean it:
UVC_EVENT_DISCONNECT, and ENODEV from the QBUF/DQBUF ioctls (both already
handled, and both left alone here).

So count the drop, say so rarely enough to stay readable, and recycle the
buffer like any other.
---
--- a/uvc-gadget.c
+++ b/uvc-gadget.c
@@ -1173,6 +1173,10 @@
     }
 }
 
+/* DashBerry: how many frames arrived incomplete. Counted rather than acted
+ * on: dropping one is normal for isochronous transfer. */
+static unsigned int uvc_dropped_frames = 0;
+
 static void uvc_v4l2_video_process()
 {
     struct v4l2_buffer ubuf;
@@ -1199,18 +1203,38 @@
 
     uvc_dev.dqbuf_count++;
 
-    /*
-        * If the dequeued buffer was marked with state ERROR by the
-        * underlying UVC driver gadget, do not queue the same to V4l2
-        * and wait for a STREAMOFF event on UVC side corresponding to
-        * set_alt(0). So, now all buffers pending at UVC end will be
-        * dequeued one-by-one and we will enter a state where we once
-        * again wait for a set_alt(1) command from the USB host side.
-        */
+    /* DashBerry: an errored buffer is a DROPPED FRAME, not a disconnect.
+     *
+     * The original code read V4L2_BUF_FLAG_ERROR as "the host is going away"
+     * — it latched uvc_shutdown_requested and, critically, RETURNED WITHOUT
+     * RE-QUEUEING THE BUFFER. That inference was already thin, and once f_uvc
+     * was taught to survive a missed isochronous slot (see the kernel patch in
+     * zero/patches/linux-custom) it became simply wrong: a missed slot sets
+     * UVC_QUEUE_DROP_INCOMPLETE, which completes that buffer with an error.
+     * Normal, expected, and not a disconnect.
+     *
+     * Leaking the buffer is what actually killed the stream. Every dropped
+     * frame permanently removed one buffer from the rotation, so the pool
+     * drained one error at a time until the host got EINVAL from poll() and
+     * "Failed to allocate a buffer". That is exactly why runs were
+     * INCONSISTENT on an unchanged card: how long one lasted was only how long
+     * it took to miss enough slots, which varies with scene, timing and
+     * temperature. It is also why raising the pool from 2 to 8 lengthened runs
+     * without fixing anything — it bought more errors before the same end.
+     *
+     * A real disconnect still arrives by the two routes that actually mean it:
+     * UVC_EVENT_DISCONNECT, and ENODEV from the QBUF/DQBUF ioctls.
+     *
+     * So: count it, mention it rarely enough to be readable, and recycle the
+     * buffer like any other.
+     */
     if (ubuf.flags & V4L2_BUF_FLAG_ERROR) {
-        uvc_shutdown_requested = true;
-        printf("UVC: Possible USB shutdown requested from Host, seen during VIDIOC_DQBUF\n");
-        return;
+        uvc_dropped_frames++;
+        if (uvc_dropped_frames == 1 || uvc_dropped_frames % 100 == 0) {
+            printf("UVC: %u incomplete frame(s) dropped so far "
+                   "(missed isochronous slots — the stream continues)\n",
+                   uvc_dropped_frames);
+        }
     }
 
     /* Queue the buffer to V4L2 domain */
